[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0f240871-7a25-57c5-a7df-58af67db238b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4 e^{- \\frac{y}{4}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$\n- **II.** $y=- 4 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$\n- **III.** $y=4 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 e^{- \\frac{y}{4}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4e^{-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{3}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4e^{0}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{4}}}\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{3}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4}{x^{\\frac{3}{4}}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{x} - \\frac{4}{x^{\\frac{3}{4}}} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 4 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{4}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 e^{- \\frac{y}{4}} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4e^{-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(- 4 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4e^{0}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(x\\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4 x \\\\ - 4 x - \\frac{4}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 4 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=4 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 e^{- \\frac{y}{4}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4e^{-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\left(4 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4e^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4 e^{\\frac{1}{4}}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4 \\left(1 - e^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=4 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f632e0f2-faa9-5ed2-a2f0-a2c4a69cfc3e",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
