    "question": {
      ":question/id": "438443c5-71c1-557b-8761-639bf0ccf74c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-6 + \\frac{3 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=- 3 x^{3} + 3 x$\n- **II.** $y=3 x^{3} + 3 x$\n- **III.** $y=- 3 x^{3} + 4 x$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 x^{3} + 3 x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 - 9 x^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6 + \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ 3 - 9 x^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3\\cdot \\left(- 3 x^{3} + 3 x\\right)}{x}-6 \\\\ 3 - 9 x^{2} & =3 - 9 x^{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=- 3 x^{3} + 3 x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3 x^{3} + 3 x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9 x^{2} + 3.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6 + \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ 9 x^{2} + 3 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3\\cdot \\left(3 x^{3} + 3 x\\right)}{x}-6 \\\\ 9 x^{2} + 3 & =9 x^{2} + 3.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3 x^{3} + 3 x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 x^{3} + 4 x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4 - 9 x^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6 + \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ 4 - 9 x^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3\\cdot \\left(- 3 x^{3} + 4 x\\right)}{x}-6 \\\\ 4 - 9 x^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}6 - 9 x^{2} \\\\ -2 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 3 x^{3} + 4 x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
