    "question": {
      ":question/id": "99a229bf-b032-5b96-9991-7559a33c6e11",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 e^{- \\frac{y}{3}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=- 3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1$\n- **II.** $y=3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$\n- **III.** $y=1 - 3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{3}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 e^{- \\frac{y}{3}} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}3e^{-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(- 3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}3e^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(x\\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}3 x e^{\\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ - 3 x e^{\\frac{1}{3}} - \\frac{3}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 e^{- \\frac{y}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}3e^{-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{3}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}3e^{0}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{3}{x} & =\\frac{3}{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=1 - 3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{3}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 e^{- \\frac{y}{3}} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}3e^{-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(1 - 3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}3e^{- \\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(x\\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3 x}{e^{\\frac{1}{3}}} \\\\ - \\frac{3 x}{e^{\\frac{1}{3}}} - \\frac{3}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=1 - 3 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
