    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5872f532-f953-5949-808c-79085dac28cb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4 + \\frac{3 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=- 2 x^{3} - 2 x + 1$\n- **II.** $y=3 x^{3} - 2 x + 1$\n- **III.** $y=- x^{3} - 2 x + 1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=- 2 x^{3} - 2 x + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 6 x^{2} - 2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 + \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ - 6 x^{2} - 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3\\cdot \\left(- 2 x^{3} - 2 x + 1\\right)}{x}+4 \\\\ - 6 x^{2} - 2 & \\overset{?}{=}- 6 x^{2} - 2 + \\frac{3}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 2 x^{3} - 2 x + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3 x^{3} - 2 x + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9 x^{2} - 2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 + \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ 9 x^{2} - 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3\\cdot \\left(3 x^{3} - 2 x + 1\\right)}{x}+4 \\\\ 9 x^{2} - 2 & \\overset{?}{=}9 x^{2} - 2 + \\frac{3}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=3 x^{3} - 2 x + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- x^{3} - 2 x + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 3 x^{2} - 2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 + \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ - 3 x^{2} - 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3\\cdot \\left(- x^{3} - 2 x + 1\\right)}{x}+4 \\\\ - 3 x^{2} - 2 & \\overset{?}{=}- 3 x^{2} - 2 + \\frac{3}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- x^{3} - 2 x + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
