    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5f1808de-6a51-55dc-ab2a-45d3b6677d3c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2 + \\frac{3 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=- x^{3} + x$\n- **II.** $y=- x^{3} + x - 1$\n- **III.** $y=x^{3} + x - 1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=- x^{3} + x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1 - 3 x^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2 + \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ 1 - 3 x^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3\\cdot \\left(- x^{3} + x\\right)}{x}-2 \\\\ 1 - 3 x^{2} & =1 - 3 x^{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=- x^{3} + x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- x^{3} + x - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1 - 3 x^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2 + \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ 1 - 3 x^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3\\cdot \\left(- x^{3} + x - 1\\right)}{x}-2 \\\\ 1 - 3 x^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{- 3 x^{3} + x - 3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{3}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- x^{3} + x - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=x^{3} + x - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 x^{2} + 1.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2 + \\frac{3 y}{x} \\\\ 3 x^{2} + 1 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3\\cdot \\left(x^{3} + x - 1\\right)}{x}-2 \\\\ 3 x^{2} + 1 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3 x^{3} + x - 3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{3}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=x^{3} + x - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
