  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1a07f198-51e7-54c7-9d88-d400fb8b97a9",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1$\n- **II.** $y=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1$\n- **III.** $y=- 2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{- \\frac{1}{2}}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x} e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x} e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2 e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{2 e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}{\\sqrt{x}} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- \\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 e^{- \\frac{y}{2}} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2e^{0}\\cdot e^{\\ln\\left(x\\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}2 x \\\\ - 2 x - \\frac{2}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "cd023ac7-5612-5b47-958e-be8acbc3671f",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
